From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 16:55:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ios: a GL memory cap that fits the whole process

Update 84's run, with the footprint finally in the log:

  [ltm 161.0s] cpu 64% of one core, 10.4 frames/s, 2006 MB
  [ltm 171.1s] cpu 65% of one core,  0.3 frames/s, 2086 MB
  [ltm 181.1s] cpu 49% of one core,  0.0 frames/s, 2070 MB
  [ltm 201.3s] cpu 63% of one core, 11.4 frames/s, 2084 MB
  [ltm 211.3s] cpu 58% of one core,  0.0 frames/s, 2071 MB

The frame rate dies exactly when the footprint sits at ~2.07 GB and
recovers when it dips. That is iOS taking memory back from a process
that is too big, not a graphics bug; to the person holding the phone it
is a freeze.

512 MiB was picked against the emulated device's RAM alone. But the
process also holds the guest's RAM (512 MB on an iPhone 4), the JIT's
code cache (512 MB by default) and QEMU's own structures, and iOS
judges the sum. Worse, the cap was never reached, so 0032's eviction --
which exists precisely to give memory back under pressure -- never ran.
The phone gave out before the budget did.

The default is now 320 MiB, which leaves the rest of the process room
and lets eviction do its job, and IT_GLES_MAX_MB overrides it so this
can be tuned against a real measurement without another emulator build.

---
  hw/arm/gles-host.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
  1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/hw/arm/gles-host.c b/hw/arm/gles-host.c
index b21b7a3..6ccc282 100644
--- a/hw/arm/gles-host.c
+++ b/hw/arm/gles-host.c
@@ -420,7 +420,32 @@ static unsigned gles_fb_w = 320, gles_fb_h = 480;   /* the iPod panel: lcd->pw/p
  * of it; past this an allocation fails with GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY, as their drivers'
  * did. Decoded PVRTC is 8-16x its size on the device, so the cap is the largest
  * RAM rather than a device's texture budget. */
-#define GLES_MAX_GPU_BYTES    ((uint64_t)512 << 20)
+/*
+ * 512 MiB was chosen against the emulated device's RAM and nothing else, which was a mistake: this process also
+ * holds the guest's RAM (512 MB on an iPhone 4), the JIT's code cache (512 MB by default) and QEMU's own
+ * structures, and iOS judges the total. Update 84's run measured 2.07 GB and the frame rate collapsed to zero
+ * whenever it sat there -- the system fighting the process for memory, which reads as a freeze. Worse, the cap
+ * was never reached, so the eviction that gives back dead apps' GL never ran: the phone gave out first.
+ *
+ * The default is now a figure that leaves the rest of the process room, and IT_GLES_MAX_MB overrides it, so the
+ * app can tune this against a real measurement without a new emulator build.
+ */
+#define GLES_DEFAULT_MAX_GPU_MB 320u
+static uint64_t gles_max_gpu_bytes(void)
+{
+    static uint64_t cap;
+
+    if (!cap) {
+        const char *v = getenv("IT_GLES_MAX_MB");
+        unsigned mb = v ? (unsigned)atoi(v) : 0;
+
+        if (mb < 64 || mb > 4096) mb = GLES_DEFAULT_MAX_GPU_MB;
+        cap = (uint64_t)mb << 20;
+        fprintf(stderr, "[gles] GL objects may hold %u MiB%s\n", mb, v && mb == atoi(v) ? " (IT_GLES_MAX_MB)" : "");
+    }
+    return cap;
+}
+#define GLES_MAX_GPU_BYTES    gles_max_gpu_bytes()
 
 /* [off, off + len) lies inside a `size`-byte buffer. Every buffer-object, index
  * and client-array range check goes through here, so none of them can wrap. */
-- 
